If you want to start imagining this, I'll give you another hint from my  
German version. My table of "articles" includes definite, indefinite, and  
"your". (Pragmatically it is convenient to treat the possessive adjective,  
or the genitive pronoun if you prefer, as an article.) This table is  
four-dimensional: the other dimensions are gender (masculine, feminine,  
neuter), case (nominative, genitive, dative and accusative) and number  
(singular, plural).

In French, you would not need neuter gender, but you would probably add  
the partitive article ("du", "de la", "des") to the  
definite-indefinite-etc. dimension.

In Russian or Greek, you might add another number between singular and  
plural.

(Wait a minute -- Russian doesn't have articles at all. Never mind!;) But  
you still need to decline adjectives and nouns in three numbers.)

I wonder, is it a coincidence that NetHack was written in English, where  
all you need is the, a, and an?

Or, if the original developers had been linguists, might they just as well  
have systematized the naming of monsters and objects using a table of  
"articles" such as "the", "a/an", "some", and "your"?

(Nobody volunteering for the full i18n rewrite?;)

